# Oatmeal — signing and release environment
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored; THIS file is not, so it must
# never hold a real secret.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Load it into a build:
#
#   set -a && source .env && set +a && pnpm tauri build
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two independent signing systems live in here. They protect different things
# and fail in completely different ways, which is why they are easy to confuse:
#
#   APPLE_*           Proves to *macOS* that the app comes from you, and that
#                     Apple has scanned it. Missing => Gatekeeper shows
#                     "unidentified developer" and users must right-click-open.
#
#   TAURI_SIGNING_*   Proves to *Oatmeal itself* that an update is genuine,
#                     using a minisign key that has nothing to do with Apple.
#                     Missing => the app silently refuses every update, and no
#                     error is shown anywhere.
#
# The GitHub Actions release workflow reads all eight as repository secrets.
# Locally you only need a subset — noted per variable below.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# === Apple: signing =========================================================

# The exact name of the signing identity in your Keychain. Not a secret: this
# string is printed inside every binary you ship.
#
# WHERE IT CAME FROM: creating a "Developer ID Application" certificate at
#   developer.apple.com/account/resources/certificates (Software > Developer ID
#   Application, G2 Sub-CA), then double-clicking the downloaded .cer.
# TO READ IT BACK:   security find-identity -v -p codesigning
# NEEDED:            local + CI
APPLE_SIGNING_IDENTITY="Developer ID Application: Your Name (TEAMID)"

# Your 10-character Apple Developer team ID. Also not a secret.
#
# WHERE IT CAME FROM: developer.apple.com/account > Membership details
# NEEDED:             local + CI
APPLE_TEAM_ID="TEAMID"

# --- CI only ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Local builds sign straight from your Keychain and never touch these two. A
# GitHub runner has no Keychain, so it needs the certificate *and* its private
# key shipped in as a base64 PKCS#12 blob.
#
# WHERE IT CAME FROM: Keychain Access > login > "My Certificates" category >
#   right-click the Developer ID certificate > Export... > File Format:
#   "Personal Information Exchange (.p12)". The "My Certificates" category
#   matters: it is the only one that lists certificates whose private key is
#   present, and exporting from plain "Certificates" silently produces a .cer
#   with no key, which CI cannot use.
# TO SET AS A SECRET:
#   base64 -i ~/Downloads/Certificates.p12 | gh secret set APPLE_CERTIFICATE
# TO CHECK THE KEY IS REALLY IN THERE:
#   openssl pkcs12 -in ~/Downloads/Certificates.p12 -nocerts -noout
APPLE_CERTIFICATE=""

# The password you invented during that .p12 export. Nothing else knows it, so
# if you lose it, re-export.
# TO SET AS A SECRET: gh secret set APPLE_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD
APPLE_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD=""


# === Apple: notarization ====================================================
# Signing and notarization are separate steps. Signing says "this is from me";
# notarization is Apple scanning the app and vouching for it. Gatekeeper wants
# both — a signed-but-unnotarized app is still rejected, with
# `spctl` reporting "source=Unnotarized Developer ID".

# The Apple ID enrolled in the Developer Program.
# NEEDED: CI, and locally only if you want `pnpm tauri build` to notarize too.
APPLE_ID=""

# An APP-SPECIFIC password, not your Apple ID password — Apple rejects the
# real one. Format: xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx
#
# WHERE IT CAME FROM: appleid.apple.com > Sign-In and Security >
#   App-Specific Passwords > generate. Revoke and regenerate freely; it is
#   scoped to this use and revoking it breaks nothing else.
# TO SET AS A SECRET: gh secret set APPLE_PASSWORD
APPLE_PASSWORD=""


# === Updater: minisign ======================================================
# Entirely separate from Apple. The public half is compiled into the app via
# `plugins.updater.pubkey` in src-tauri/tauri.conf.json; this is the private
# half that signs each release.

# Tauri accepts either the key's *contents* or a *path to it*. Prefer the path
# locally — then no key material is ever copied into a dotfile.
#
# WHERE IT CAME FROM: pnpm exec tauri signer generate --ci -p "" -w ~/.tauri/oatmeal.key
# TO SET AS A SECRET:  gh secret set TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY < ~/.tauri/oatmeal.key
#
# !! BACK THIS FILE UP. It is not recoverable and not re-derivable. Lose it and
# !! every already-installed copy of Oatmeal will reject every future release,
# !! with no way to recover except making users reinstall by hand.
TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY="$HOME/.tauri/oatmeal.key"

# Empty if the key was generated with `-p ""` as above. Must still be *set*, or
# Tauri prompts for it and a non-interactive build hangs rather than failing.
#
# TO SET AS A SECRET (note the pipe — `--body ""` makes gh wait on stdin
# forever instead of storing an empty value):
#   printf '' | gh secret set TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY_PASSWORD
TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY_PASSWORD=""


# === Notes that cost real time to learn =====================================
#
# 1. The Developer ID intermediate CA is NOT preinstalled on macOS and does NOT
#    come with your certificate. Without it, `codesign` fails with "unable to
#    build chain to self-signed root" and `security find-identity -v` reports
#    zero valid identities — while `security verify-cert` succeeds, because it
#    may fetch the intermediate over the network and find-identity will not.
#
#      curl -fsS -o /tmp/DeveloperIDG2CA.cer \
#        https://www.apple.com/certificateauthority/DeveloperIDG2CA.cer
#      security import /tmp/DeveloperIDG2CA.cer -k login.keychain
#
#    The release workflow does this on the runner for the same reason.
#
# 2. Setting `pubkey` in tauri.conf.json turns updater artifacts on, so a bare
#    `pnpm tauri build` now fails with "a public key has been found, but no
#    private key". Source this file first and it is fine.
#
# 3. Apple's timestamp server is a hard dependency of Developer ID signing and
#    it does flake: "The timestamp service is not available" is transient — just
#    retry. Never silence it with `--timestamp=none`; notarization requires the
#    timestamp.
#
# 4. An EMPTY variable is not the same as an ABSENT one. Tauri tests whether
#    APPLE_CERTIFICATE is *set*, so `APPLE_CERTIFICATE=""` plus `set -a` makes a
#    local build try to import a zero-byte certificate and fail with:
#
#      security: SecKeychainItemImport: One or more parameters passed to a
#      function were not valid.
#      failed to bundle project: failed codesign application
#
#    which reads like a broken certificate rather than an empty variable. Keep
#    the CI-only pair commented out in a local .env rather than blank. The same
#    applies to APPLE_ID/APPLE_PASSWORD: absent means "sign but skip
#    notarization", which is a valid local build.
